## Proof

Let $E$ be a Euclidean space with $\dim E \le 3$. For a nonempty $A\subseteq E$, recall
$$S(A)=\bigl\{\,t a+(1-t)b : a,b\in A,\; t\in[0,1]\,\bigr\},$$
where the convention that a one-point set is a closed segment means $A\subseteq S(A)$ (take $a=b$). Hence
$$A_0\subseteq A_1\subseteq A_2\subseteq\cdots.$$

**Lemma 1.** $A_n$ is exactly the set of convex combinations of at most $2^n$ points of $A_0$.

*Proof.* By induction. For $n=0$: $A_0$ is the set of convex combinations of $1\le 2^0$ point. Assume $A_{n-1}$ is the set of convex combinations of at most $2^{n-1}$ points of $A_0$. A point of $A_n=S(A_{n-1})$ has the form $t p+(1-t)q$ with $p,q\in A_{n-1}$. By hypothesis $p$ is a convex combination of $\le 2^{n-1}$ points and $q$ of $\le 2^{n-1}$ points, so $t p+(1-t)q$ is a convex combination of $\le 2^{n-1}+2^{n-1}=2^n$ points of $A_0$. Conversely, any convex combination $\sum_{i=1}^{m}\alpha_i a_i$ with $m\le 2^n$ and $\sum\alpha_i=1$, $\alpha_i\ge 0$, can be split into two groups of size $\le 2^{n-1}$: let $P=\sum_{i\in I}\alpha_i$, and write
$$\sum_i \alpha_i a_i = P\Bigl(\sum_{i\in I}\tfrac{\alpha_i}{P}a_i\Bigr)+(1-P)\Bigl(\sum_{i\notin I}\tfrac{\alpha_i}{1-P}a_i\Bigr),$$
with $I$ chosen so $|I|,|\overline{I}|\le 2^{n-1}$; each parenthesized term lies in $A_{n-1}$ by hypothesis, so the whole lies in $S(A_{n-1})=A_n$. (Degenerate cases $P=0$ or $P=1$ reduce to a single group and land in $A_{n-1}\subseteq A_n$.) $\square$

In particular, **$A_2$ is the set of convex combinations of at most $4$ points of $A_0$.**

**Lemma 2 (Carathéodory).** If $\dim E=d$, every point of $\operatorname{conv}(A_0)$ is a convex combination of at most $d+1$ points of $A_0$.

*Proof.* Take a point $x\in\operatorname{conv}(A_0)$ written as $x=\sum_{i=1}^{m}\alpha_i a_i$ with $m$ minimal. If $m>d+1$, then $m-1\ge d+1>d$, so the $m-1$ vectors $a_2-a_1,\dots,a_m-a_1$ are linearly dependent: there exist $\beta_2,\dots,\beta_m$, not all zero, with $\sum_{i=2}^{m}\beta_i(a_i-a_1)=0$. Set $\beta_1=-\sum_{i=2}^m\beta_i$, so $\sum_{i=1}^m\beta_i a_i=0$ and $\sum\beta_i=0$. For $\tau\ge0$,
$$x=\sum_i(\alpha_i-\tau\beta_i)a_i,\qquad \sum_i(\alpha_i-\tau\beta_i)=1.$$
Choose $\tau=\min\{\alpha_i/\beta_i:\beta_i>0\}>0$; then all coefficients stay $\ge0$ and at least one becomes $0$, reducing $m$ — contradicting minimality. Hence $m\le d+1$. $\square$

**Conclusion.** Since $\dim E\le 3$, we have $d+1\le 4$. By Lemma 2,
$$\operatorname{conv}(A_0)=\{\text{convex combinations of at most }4\text{ points of }A_0\}.$$
By Lemma 1 this set is exactly $A_2$. Thus
$$A_2=\operatorname{conv}(A_0).$$
The convex hull is convex, so $S(\operatorname{conv}(A_0))=\operatorname{conv}(A_0)$, giving $A_3=S(A_2)=A_2$. By induction $A_n=A_2$ for all $n\ge 2$. (The inclusion $A_n\subseteq\operatorname{conv}(A_0)$ for all $n$ is immediate since $S$ of a subset of a convex set stays in that convex set, and $A_0\subseteq\operatorname{conv}(A_0)$; combined with the increasing chain this gives equality from $n=2$ onward.)

Therefore $A_2=A_3=\cdots$. $\blacksquare$

